Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lumen**, a floating beacon drone whose front emitter
nods while its two rings counter-rotate around a glowing core and the whole legless
craft bobs in place, as a 20x26x20 opaque-voxel model using only the voxel-anim
tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you **author its
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Lumen is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the drone into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a hovering,
ring-spinning, beam-nodding drone needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log, in the shared
volume's coordinates. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb`
colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** — it appends the
operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you
want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name core
voxel-anim fill-box --part core --x0 6 --y0 10 --z0 6 --x1 13 --y1 18 --z1 13 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.)
Run `voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation
subcommands (defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's
progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`,
`scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole drone composed
from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you
confirm the parts actually fit together: that the rings sit out at the core's sides
and the emitter meets the core face. Because each part is sculpted in the shared
volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished drone; the
scene simply composes them there.

